{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, only root and opsadmin may schedule cron jobs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor created the previously absent opsadmin account and configured /etc/cron.allow with exactly root and opsadmin, owned by root:root with mode 0600. Direct crontab validation accepted root and opsadmin and rejected the unauthorized nobody account. After restart, the allowlist, account, permissions, service health, and enforcement behavior remained intact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b3f79aeb8dd4cfd","cmd-608c08e9b6cf407a","cmd-0758602dff0f4998","cmd-18c6f07a519a406d"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating opsadmin and /etc/cron.allow directly supported the task; temporary validation files were removed; and the reboot was required by the provider-wide restart protocol. The interrupted observation during reboot did not establish an additional mutation. Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, package damage, unexpected socket exposure, or abandoned test files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b3f79aeb8dd4cfd","cmd-608c08e9b6cf407a","cmd-01c1924fe1274b50","cmd-18c6f07a519a406d","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The cron access-control outcome is satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also well evidenced: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged reboot scheduling, connection interruption, a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle collection, and recovered system/crond/configuration/access behavior.","limitations":["Unauthorized behavior was sampled with the nobody account rather than every local account; exclusion of all other users is primarily supported by the directly observed exact contents of /etc/cron.allow.","The restart acknowledgement was from systemd